<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ctesias</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. c. 400 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a> · Also known as Ctesias of Cnidus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ctesias of Cnidus was a Greek physician and historian of the late 5th and early 4th centuries BCE who served at the Persian court of Artaxerxes II. He wrote a history of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>, the Persica, and a work on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, the Indica, both now known only from fragments and summaries. Ancient and modern readers have judged them full of legend and error, but they shaped Greek ideas of the East.</p></div>
